---
/**
 * FFL Firewall terms. Source: brand/ffl-firewall/terms/ffl-firewall-terms-final.md,
 * published verbatim for FFLF-FIREWALL-V1-S3B-BUY (Karp amendment 7c5cd47).
 * "The shared reputation feed" section: Brandy's terms lines, verbatim, from
 * TERMS-feed-lines-2026-11-19.md (Paul approved 1:41 PM ET; ships with S6E, Paul 1:35 PM ET).
 * FFLFW-TERMS-DEFAULT-SHARE (Karp-cleared 2026-11-23): T0, T2, T3 and the no-trial line (option A);
 * T1 (Brandy's no-choice revision), Karp-cleared 11:05 AM ET.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="FFL Firewall terms | Coriolis, LLC"
  description="Terms for FFL Firewall, a managed security service for FFL dealers, delivered as a WooCommerce plugin, provided by Coriolis, LLC."
  path="/ffl-firewall/terms"
  emailCapture={false}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 sm:py-16">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">FFL Firewall terms</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-zinc-500">Effective the day FFL Firewall billing launches.</p>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Who provides FFL Firewall</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">FFL Firewall is a managed security service for FFL dealers, provided by Coriolis, LLC and delivered as a WooCommerce plugin. It includes three services: FFL Firewall · ACTIVE DEFENSE, FFL Firewall · OVERWATCH and FFL Firewall · RECON. If you have a question about these terms or about the plugin, write to Coriolis support at support@coriolisagency.com.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">What it does</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">At checkout, FFL Firewall checks each product in the cart by UPC against the zip code the shopper entered. When that check finds an item is prohibited for the zip, the plugin acts on the rule you set in its settings: it removes the item from the cart and shows the shopper a short message explaining why, or it lets the item through and flags the order. A flag means the order gets an order note and a Flagged label in your WooCommerce Orders screen, and Coriolis sends you no email about it.</p>
        <p class="mt-2">For handguns in roster states, the Roster states setting gives you three choices: remove the item from the cart, allow it and flag the order, or allow it with no flag. When an item is allowed and flagged, the shopper sees nothing about the flag at checkout. Every removal, flag and shopper notice is listed on the plugin's Log tab, so you can look back at what the check did.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Your responsibility</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">FFL Firewall is a tool that helps you catch restricted items at checkout. You're still responsible for what you sell and what you ship, and this is not legal advice. The check can miss an item or flag one that's fine, so keep your own review in place and look at every order yourself before anything ships.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Price, billing and cancelling</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">FFL Firewall costs $79 a month, and your first month is charged the day you sign up, with no trial period. You're charged monthly in advance, by card, through Stripe Checkout. If a monthly payment fails, the checks keep running for 7 days from the day it failed; if it's still unpaid after that, they stop until you pay, and they start again once you do. You can cancel at any time; when you do, the checks keep running until the end of the month you've already paid for, and then they stop, so carts go through checkout without the product check from that point on. We don't refund partial months.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">What the plugin sends to Coriolis</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">To run the product check, the plugin sends only the UPC of each product in the cart and the zip code the shopper entered at checkout. To check your license, it sends your license key and your site's URL. It never sends the shopper's name.</p>
        <p class="mt-2">When FFL Firewall · ACTIVE DEFENSE is on, the dealer embed script also sends anonymized, aggregate traffic signals to Coriolis, LLC, and the shared reputation feed section below covers what your store reports to the feed.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">The shared reputation feed</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">Stores that install FFL Firewall from now on are in the shared reputation feed by default while FFL Firewall · ACTIVE DEFENSE is switched on and their license is active; stores that already had FFL Firewall installed keep the feed setting they had, and if you never made a choice, the feed stays off until you switch it on. Either way, you can switch the feed on or off at any time. One switch covers both directions, so being in the feed means your store reports the bots its checks catch and receives a list of bots that other FFL Firewall stores have reported. The feed only works while FFL Firewall · ACTIVE DEFENSE is on, and it needs an active FFL Firewall license.</p>
        <p class="mt-2">While your store is in the feed, the plugin sends Coriolis, LLC signals about the bots your store catches, and nothing about your shoppers. The signals are salted hashes of the IP addresses and IPv6 networks the bots came from, the bot's user agent when a request fails the user agent check, and the type of pattern caught, such as a honeypot form field or a run of failed payments. Raw IP addresses stay on your store, because the plugin hashes them before anything is sent. Rate-limit hits stay on your store too and are never sent to the feed. Shoppers are never named or identified, and the plugin sends no names, emails, addresses, orders, cart contents or payment details.</p>
        <p class="mt-2">Coriolis, LLC uses feed signals only for the shared list, and your store's reports are deleted after 30 days. To leave the feed, untick &quot;Join the shared reputation feed&quot; in the Shared reputation feed section of the FFL Firewall · ACTIVE DEFENSE tab and click &quot;Save feed setting&quot;; once the feed is off, your store stops sending signals and stops using the shared list.</p>
        <p class="mt-2">If the feed stops a real visitor on your store, you can mark that visitor as Not a bot from the plugin. The visitor is unblocked on your store right away, and Coriolis reviews whether to take them off the network list. Coriolis decides the result of each review and doesn't commit to a particular result or a time for it.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">When the check can't run</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">Sometimes the check can't run at all, for example when the checking service is unavailable or your license key is invalid. In that case the item passes, the shopper sees no message and can keep checking out, and the order is flagged with an order note and a Flagged label so you know to review it yourself before you ship.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Limit of liability</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">Coriolis's total liability to you for anything arising from FFL Firewall is capped at the fees you paid for it in the 12 months before the claim.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Changes to these terms</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">If we change these terms, we'll email you at least 30 days before the change takes effect.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Governing law</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">These terms are governed by the laws of the State of South Carolina, and any dispute will be heard in Greenville County, South Carolina.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
